Why GPLv3: the binary wheels statically link ORB-SLAM3, which is GPLv3, so the
distributed package is a GPLv3 work. The binding source in src/ and the Python
package in pyorbslam3/ are released under the same license to keep things simple.

Third-party components compiled into the wheels (or fetched and compiled by a
source install), with their licenses:

  ORB-SLAM3        GPL-3.0        https://github.com/UZ-SLAMLab/ORB_SLAM3
                                  (commit 4452a3c4, plus docker/patches/)
  DBoW2 (modified, bundled in ORB-SLAM3)   BSD-3-Clause
  g2o   (modified, bundled in ORB-SLAM3)   BSD-2-Clause (core); see Thirdparty/g2o/license-bsd.txt
  Sophus (bundled in ORB-SLAM3)            MIT
  Pangolin 0.9     MIT            https://github.com/stevenlovegrove/Pangolin
  OpenCV 4.10      Apache-2.0     https://github.com/opencv/opencv   (static, minimal module set)
  Eigen 3.4        MPL-2.0        https://eigen.tuxfamily.org
  Boost.Serialization   BSL-1.0   https://www.boost.org             (shared, bundled by auditwheel)
  OpenSSL 1.1      Apache-2.0 / OpenSSL license                     (shared, bundled by auditwheel)
  GLEW, GLU        BSD / MIT, SGI Free Software License B           (shared, bundled by auditwheel)
  pybind11         BSD-3-Clause   https://github.com/pybind/pybind11
  CMake modernization patch for ORB-SLAM3 (docker/patches/0001-*):
                   MIT, from PRBonn's ORB-SLAM-Python project

The ORB vocabulary (ORBvoc.txt) is downloaded at runtime from the ORB-SLAM3
repository and is not part of this package.
